          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>First,  we simplify the given expression using the following identities:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}, \\qquad\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}]</span></p><p>Substituting the above into our expression, we get</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\cos x\\csc x\\sec^{2} x|= \\cos x \u22c5 (1)/(\\sin x) \u22c5 (1)/(\\cos^{2} x); = (\\cos x)/(\\sin x\\cos^{2} x); = (\\cos x)/(\\sin x\\cos x\\cos x); = (\\cos x)/(\\sin x\\cos x\\cos x); = (1)/(\\sin x\\cos x).]</span></p><p>Now, we recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin{2x}]</span></p><p>Substituting the above into our expression, we finally get</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (1)/(\\sin x\\cos x)|= (2)/(2\\sin x\\cos x); = (2)/(\\sin 2x); = 2\\csc 2x.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "First, we simplify the given expression using the following identities:\n\n[MATH: \\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}, \\qquad\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}]\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression, we get\n\n[MATH: \\cos x\\csc x\\sec^{2} x|= \\cos x \u22c5 (1)/(\\sin x) \u22c5 (1)/(\\cos^{2} x); = (\\cos x)/(\\sin x\\cos^{2} x); = (\\cos x)/(\\sin x\\cos x\\cos x); = (\\cos x)/(\\sin x\\cos x\\cos x); = (1)/(\\sin x\\cos x).]\n\nNow, we recall the double-angle formula for sine:\n[MATH: 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin{2x}]\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression, we finally get\n\n[MATH: (1)/(\\sin x\\cos x)|= (2)/(2\\sin x\\cos x); = (2)/(\\sin 2x); = 2\\csc 2x.]",
