    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nThe double-angle formula\n[MATH: \\sin{2x}=2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}]\ncan be used to simplify complicated trigonometric expressions. For instance, suppose we want to simplify the expression\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{2\\cos^2{x}}.]\n\nContent:\nUsing the double-angle formula, we can simplify the given expression, as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (\\sin 2x)/(2\\cos^{2} x)|= (2\\sin x\\cos x)/(2\\cos x\\cos x); = (2\\cos x \u22c5 \\sin x)/(2\\cos x \u22c5 \\cos x); = (\\sin x)/(\\cos x); = \\tan x]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: \\dfrac{\\sin^2{x}}{\\sin{2x}}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we recall the double-angle formula for sine:\n[MATH: \\sin{2x} = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}]\n\nSubstituting the above into the given expression, we get\n\n[MATH: (\\sin^{2} x)/(\\sin 2x)|= (\\sin x\\sin x)/(2\\sin x\\cos x); = (\\sin x\\sin x)/(2\\sin x\\cos x); = (\\sin x)/(2\\cos x); = (1)/(2)\\tan x.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nWhich of the following expressions is equivalent to [MATH: (\\sin x \u2212 \\cos x)^{2}?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 2\\sin 2x]\nb. [MATH: 1 + \\sin 2x]\nc. [MATH: \\sin 2x \u2212 1]\nd. [MATH: 1 \u2212 \\sin 2x]\ne. [MATH: \\sin 2x]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (2\\cos^{2} x)/(\\sin 2x) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2\\cot x]\nb. [MATH: 2\\cot^{2} x]\nc. [MATH: \\cot x]\nd. [MATH: \\cot^{2} x]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(2)\\cot x]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Expressions Containing the Cosecant of a Double Angle\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: 2\\tan x \\csc 2x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, recall that\n[MATH: \\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}, \\qquad \\csc 2x = \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}\\sin 2x}.]\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for sine,\n[MATH: \\sin{2x} = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x},]\nwe simplify the expression as follows:\n\n[MATH: 2\\tan x\\csc 2x|= 2 \u22c5 (\\sin x)/(\\cos x) \u22c5 (1)/(\\sin 2x); = (2\\sin x)/(\\cos x) \u22c5 (1)/(2\\sin x\\cos x); = (2\\sin x)/(\\cos x) \u22c5 (1)/(2\\sin x\\cos x); = (1)/(\\cos^{2} x); = \\sec^{2} x]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: 2\\sin x\\csc 2x.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \\sec^{2} x]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(\\sec x)]\nc. [MATH: \\sec x]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 \\sec^{2} x]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 \\sec x]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: 2\\cos x\\csc 2x.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \\csc^{2} x]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(\\csc x)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 \\csc x]\nd. [MATH: \\csc x]\ne. [MATH: 2\\csc x]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Advanced Trigonometric Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine\n\nExample Prompt:\nWrite [MATH: \\cos{x}\\csc{x}\\sec^2{x}] as a single trigonometric function.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we simplify the given expression using the following identities:\n\n[MATH: \\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}, \\qquad\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}]\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression, we get\n\n[MATH: \\cos x\\csc x\\sec^{2} x|= \\cos x \u22c5 (1)/(\\sin x) \u22c5 (1)/(\\cos^{2} x); = (\\cos x)/(\\sin x\\cos^{2} x); = (\\cos x)/(\\sin x\\cos x\\cos x); = (\\cos x)/(\\sin x\\cos x\\cos x); = (1)/(\\sin x\\cos x).]\n\nNow, we recall the double-angle formula for sine:\n[MATH: 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin{2x}]\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression, we finally get\n\n[MATH: (1)/(\\sin x\\cos x)|= (2)/(2\\sin x\\cos x); = (2)/(\\sin 2x); = 2\\csc 2x.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nWhich of the following is equivalent to [MATH: (1)/(2)\\cot x + (1)/(2)\\tan x?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 \\csc 2x]\nb. [MATH: \\csc 2x]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(2)\\csc 2x]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 2\\csc 2x]\ne. [MATH: 2\\csc 2x]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\\sin x \u22c5 \\sin^{2} 2x)/(4\\cos^{2} x) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2\\sin x]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(4)\\sin x]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(2)\\sin^{2} x]\nd. [MATH: \\sin^{3} x]\ne. [MATH: \\sin^{2} x]"
